          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is I10 a complete code, or does it need a fourth character?",
          "answer": "Yes, I10 is a complete ICD-10-CM code. It stands for essential, or primary, hypertension. This code is unusual because most ICD-10-CM categories require a fourth, fifth, or even sixth character to be complete and billable (Source: `coding-hypertension-i10`).\n\nIn general, ICD-10-CM codes can be three to seven characters long. The first three characters form the \"category\" of the code. If more characters are needed, they add further detail about the condition, such as its cause, where it is in the body, or how severe it is. A decimal point is placed after the third character if additional characters follow (Source: `coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions`).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
